Article 28
Every Child has the right to:
a. live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth, and receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. obtain a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under the guidance of parents or guardians;
d. know their parents, be raised, and be cared for by their own parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules necessitating separation for the best interests of the Child;
e. have their growth and development guaranteed, not be neglected, by being cared for or appointed as a foster child or adopted as an adopted child by others;
f. obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
g. obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development and the Child's level of intelligence in accordance with the Child's interests and talents;
h. receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, educational personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
i. obtain special education and obtain extraordinary education;
1. special education for Children with Disabilities;
k. obtain rehabilitation, social assistance, and maintenance of social welfare standards for Children with disabilities; express and have their opinions heard; receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for their self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety; rest and utilize leisure time; associate with Children of the same age; play, recreate, and create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development; receive protection from discriminatory treatment;
m. receive protection from exploitation both economic and sexual, neglect, cruelty, Violence, and abuse, injustice, misuse in political activities, and sexual crimes;
n. receive protection from involvement in armed conflict, social unrest, and events containing elements of violence and warfare; obtain protection from being targets of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhumane punishment; meet directly and maintain personal relations regularly with both parents in cases where the Child is cared for not by their parents; receive care, maintenance, education and protection for the growth and development process from both parents in accordance with the Child's abilities, talents, and interests; obtain financial support for living from both parents; obtain freedom in accordance with legal rules; in cases of arrest, detention, or criminal imprisonment of a Child carried out as a last resort, and for Children deprived of their freedom receive humane treatment and placement of the Child separated from adults; obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of legal proceedings; self-defense and justice before an objective and impartial Children's court in closed sessions to the public.

Article 30
Every Child has the right to receive Special Protection.
(1) Children who require Special Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. Children in emergency situations;
b. Children in conflict with the law;
c. Children from minority and isolated groups;
d. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. Children who are victims of abuse of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances;
f. Children who are victims of pornography;
g. Children with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)/acquired immuno deficiency syndrome (AIDS);
h. Children who are victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. Children who are victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. Children who are victims of sexual crimes;
k. Children who are victims of terrorist networks;
l. Children with Disabilities;
m. Children who are victims of abuse and neglect;
n. Children with deviant social behavior; and
o. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
(3) The forms of Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 74
The Regional-level Puspa Forum as referred to in paragraph (1) of Article 73 has the duty to coordinate and synergize community participation in the implementation of the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection at the Regional level.

In carrying out the coordinating duty as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional-level Puspa Forum shall conduct:
 a. communication;
 b. planning of time and activity schedule;
 c. flexibility in changes; and
 d. control.

In carrying out the synergizing duty as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional-level Puspa Forum shall conduct:
 a. alignment of vision, mission, and objectives;
 b. understanding of strengths and talents of team members;
 c. similarity of concepts and ways of thinking;
 d. good planning;
 e. clear division of work and roles; and
 f. efforts to build honest and mutually open communication.

In carrying out the duties as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional-level Puspa Forum shall perform the functions of:
 a. formulation of strategies and action plans of the Regional-level Puspa Forum;
 b. providing input for the formulation of policies, programs, and activities related to Gender Equality, protection of women's rights, fulfillment of Children's rights, and Special Protection for Children;
 c. implementing advocacy, socialization, training, and cooperation;
 d. conducting assistance in the development of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in Regions and Villages, including women-friendly and child-caring Villages;
 e. monitoring the progress of implementation of community participation for the welfare of women and Children; and
 f. implementing evaluation and reporting.

Article 47
Provision of services at UPTD PPA in the context of (1) Protection services for women victims of violence and for Children requiring Special Protection includes:
a. public complaints;
b. victim outreach;
c. case management;
d. temporary shelter;
e. mediation; and
f. victim assistance.
(2) UPTD PPA in providing services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with service standards that have been established by laws and regulations.

Article 17
(1) Every business actor, in fulfilling women's rights in general, has the obligations and responsibilities:
   a. to protect female workers from threats of violence, discrimination, harassment, and intimidation;
   b. to provide job vacancies for women;
   c. to provide opportunities for women to be employed according to the type of work that can be performed by women;
   d. to include all employees as participants in national health insurance and employment insurance in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
   e. to comply with all applicable rules in fulfilling women's rights; and
   f. to provide a lactation room.
(2) In addition to the obligations and responsibilities of business actors in general as referred to in paragraph (1), specifically:
   a. every owner/manager of a business in the field of industry and trade has the obligations and responsibilities:
      1) not to assign female employees who are pregnant or breastfeeding to work at night;
      2) to provide a lactation room; and
      3) to designate certain workplaces/work rooms as smoke-free zones.
   b. every manager/owner of a business in the field of hotels, lodging, homestays, guesthouses, resorts, tourist cottages, and other similar accommodation is prohibited from:
      1) allowing women to stay in one room with men, unless they have a husband-and-wife relationship or are biological children;
      2) allowing a man to visit the room of a female guest/occupant, unless they have a family relationship;
      3) allowing a woman to visit the room of a male guest/occupant, unless they have a family relationship; and
      4) providing massage and spa services without permission from the Regional Government.
   c. every owner/manager of entertainment and recreation venues is prohibited from providing a place for the practice of prostitution or potentially leading to prostitution.
(3) Every business actor/owner/manager who does not fulfill the obligations and prohibitions as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) may be subject to administrative sanctions.
(4) Administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (3) consist of: verbal warning, written warning, administrative fine, temporary closure of business activities, or revocation of business license which is the authority of the Regional Government. The administrative fine as referred to in paragraph (4) letter c is at most Rp50,000,000,- (fifty million (S) rupiah).

